// BASICStudio.fbsettings — BASICStudio.app, built by fbbuild (Bobby, 2026-11-18:
// "Fbbuild is how everything is to be built").
//
//   fbbuild build BASICStudio/BASICStudio.fbsettings --platform mac
//
// and, app through staging through installer in one line:
//
//   ~/AIResearch/FlexInstall/Scripts/build-app-and-installer.sh \
//       BASICStudio/BASICStudio.fbsettings --sign auto \
//       --project ~/AIResearch/BBCBasic/Code/flexinstall/BASICStudio.flexproj
//
// `--project` is needed because the installer project sits in `BBCBasic`, which is
// neither this product's repository nor under git at all; the script's own search looks
// in `<repository root>/Code/flexinstall`, and this repository's root *is* `Code`. Worth
// moving, and Bobby's call rather than a build script's.
//
// **The app's own recipe builds it**, not a scheme named here. Until 2026-11-26 this
// file named the package's `BASICStudio` scheme, which builds the *executable*: the
// bundle around it was assembled separately by the CodeBuilder job, so `fbbuild` left
// no `.app` and nothing that stages or installs applications could drive it. BASIC
// Studio was the one product of the seventeen whose installer had to be rebuilt from an
// older staged app for exactly that reason. `Scripts/CIBuild-BASICStudio.sh` is the
// recipe that was already there and already right: it moves the committed
// BASICStudio.xcodeproj aside so xcodebuild reads the package, builds Release for
// arm64, assembles the bundle with the version settings project.yml carries, makes
// AppIcon.icns from the ten PNGs in AppIcon.iconset, copies every resource bundle into
// Contents/Resources where Xcode's `Bundle.module` looks for them, embeds every
// framework and dylib the executable loads, fails if it loads one it does not carry,
// and signs inside out. None of that is a scheme and a set of arguments, so this is a
// `build: .script`.
//
// **Not project.yml's app target.** The spec describes an Xcode application target, and
// the sources reach their demos, fonts and UserDocs.zip through `Bundle.module`, which
// exists only for a SwiftPM target: built from the spec, `Bundle.module` in
// UserDocumentationPane.swift resolves to SwiftTerm's internal one and the build stops
// on "'module' is inaccessible due to 'internal' protection level". The package build
// gives every target its own accessor, and Xcode's looks in Contents/Resources — where
// a signed app keeps its bundles — unlike the one `swift build` generates, which looks
// beside the executable and at the absolute build path of the Mac that built it.
//
// **Signed ad hoc by the recipe, with Developer ID by whoever ships it.** The script
// signs ad hoc so a fresh clone builds; the CodeBuilder job and FlexInstall's
// `signPayload` both re-sign inside out with the Developer ID.
//
// **More platforms later.** A script build runs on this Mac and builds for the Mac
// only, so another platform means another settings file rather than another line here.
schemaVersion: 1

platforms: (mac: true)
configurations: (default: "Release", build: "Release")
mac: (architectures: .arm64)

apple: (bundleIdentifier: "com.aibasic.BASICStudio")

build: .script
script: (run: "../Scripts/CIBuild-BASICStudio.sh")
products: ["../Build/CIBuild/BASICStudio.app"]
